{
  "narrative_body": "Ukraine asked Turkey this week to host a direct meeting between Zelensky and Putin — a move that marks a shift from complaining about stalled American mediation to building around it.\n\nThe request, conveyed formally by Andrii Sybiha, the foreign minister, reflects Ukraine's reading that Washington's peace channel has gone cold while the US-Iran war absorbs American attention. Zelensky said openly that a meeting with the American president was unlikely until the Iran conflict ends, and called it \"disrespectful\" that US envoys kept visiting Moscow without stopping in Kyiv. Kyiv said it would accept any neutral venue except Russia or Belarus. The Kremlin's response was to say Putin would only meet Zelensky to \"finalize a deal\" — not to negotiate — a condition that requires Ukraine to accept Russian terms before talks begin, and that shifts blame for the impasse toward Moscow.\n\nEven as Ukraine was reshaping its diplomatic posture, Russia was bombing Dnipro. A three-wave assault over more than 20 hours on April 25 sent 619 drones and 47 missiles at Ukrainian cities — Ukraine downed 580 of the drones and 30 of the missiles, but 10 people died, including in apartment blocks struck while rescuers worked. For the second week in a row, Russia launched a major sustained barrage; the attacks are settling into a new higher baseline rather than remaining episodic. The most significant new development was outside Ukraine's borders: a drone fragment struck an electricity pole in Romania. No one was hurt, but NATO territory was reached, and NATO's non-escalatory response accepted the fact rather than contested it.\n\nUkraine's outward diplomacy extended well beyond Turkey. Zelensky made his second visit to Saudi Arabia in under a month, meeting Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman on air defense, energy, and infrastructure. He then flew to Baku, where he signed six agreements with Ilham Aliyev, Azerbaijan's president, including security cooperation. Ukraine has now signed 10-year defense export agreements with Saudi Arabia, the UAE, and Qatar, centered on drone warfare expertise. The significance is not merely commercial: Ukraine is no longer only an aid recipient but a security-knowledge exporter, selling the lessons of two years of drone warfare to Gulf states seeking to build their own capacity.\n\nIn Europe, the security architecture debate advanced to institutional form. EU leaders discussed invoking Article 42.7 — the EU's mutual defense clause — independent of NATO. Emmanuel Macron argued the provision is \"stronger than NATO's Article 5.\" Donald Tusk, the Polish prime minister, warned Russia could attack a NATO member \"within months.\" Washington, meanwhile, was reportedly sorting NATO allies into \"good\" and \"bad\" categories based on compliance with US Iran policy, with Poland and the Baltic states marked as exemplary and Spain and the UK facing potential troop-relocation threats. For Ukraine, which is building a security architecture premised on Europe rather than Washington as its primary guarantor, the Article 42.7 debate is the week's most telling European development.\n\nOn the military front, General Oleksandr Syrskyi, the commander-in-chief, visited the Pokrovsk sector — the most heavily attacked stretch of the front, with 688 Russian attacks in April — and ordered a logistics inspection to be completed by May 20. He met NATO and EU military committee chairs to present Ukraine's critical needs, and claimed Russia can no longer actively counter Ukrainian drones because its air defenses are running short of missiles. The claim cannot be independently verified, and Syrskyi himself acknowledged that Russia is advancing \"nearly the entire front\" — a tension in how Ukraine's military leadership is framing the same operational situation to different audiences.\n\nAt the Kyiv Security Forum, Kyrylo Budanov, head of the presidential office, declared Ukraine \"definitely not losing\" and announced that AI-autonomous drone systems are near-ready and will \"surprise\" Russia. The forum is a public platform for an international audience, and the announcement reads as strategic communication as much as operational briefing — the gap between aspiration and readiness cannot be resolved at current evidence levels. What is analytically valid in Mr Budanov's argument: jamming-resistant autonomous systems would directly address the electronic warfare problem he described, and the drone industrial base Ukraine has built makes the capability at least plausible.\n\nOn the 40th anniversary of the Chernobyl disaster, Rafael Grossi, the IAEA's director general, signed an energy reconstruction agreement with Ukraine and called for urgent repairs to the protective shelter damaged by a Russian drone in February 2025. The European Bank for Reconstruction and Development identified a €500 million need to prevent permanent damage; 92 Russian drones have flown within 5 kilometres of the shelter since June 2024. Zelensky called Russia's behaviour near Chernobyl \"nuclear terrorism\" and, for the first time, linked Zaporizhzhia's return to Ukrainian control explicitly to peace terms — adding the plant's fate to the list of conditions Ukraine is staking out ahead of any negotiations.\n\nAt home, the wartime social contract is under pressure across several fronts at once. A government petition cited 36 firearms homicides in 2021 against 1,052 in 2024 — a 29-fold wartime increase attributed to black-market weapons proliferation, filed in the wake of the April 18 Kyiv shooting that killed six. One petition called for legalized concealed carry; another called for dismissing Interior Minister Ihor Klymenko, who said publicly that \"police are not trained to fight.\" Mr Budanov publicly opposed gun legalization, fixing the government's position against the civic demand. Separate petitions called for early retirement at 55 for mobilized service members without combat veteran status who have served 18 months or more, and for military pay reform raising base salaries and fixing pay continuity for personnel on medical treatment — identifying logistics and support troops as underrecognized and underpaid relative to frontline combat personnel. Energy restoration costs are now appearing in civic fiscal discourse too: one petition estimated UAH 278 billion to repair the grid before winter. None of these petitions has reached the threshold for a mandatory Cabinet response, but together they trace the outline of fiscal and institutional pressure still building beneath the surface of the war.",
  "other_stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Ukraine pushes for direct Zelensky–Putin summit, asks Turkey to host talks as US mediation stalls",
      "summary": "Ukraine formally asked Turkey to host a meeting between Presidents Zelensky and Putin as peace negotiations stalled amid the US-Iran war absorbing Washington's attention. FM Sybiha said Kyiv would accept any neutral venue except Russia or Belarus, while Zelensky simultaneously called Trump envoys' repeated Moscow visits without Kyiv stops 'disrespectful.' Trump confirmed he was holding calls with both leaders."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Syrskyi conducts Pokrovsk frontline visit, orders logistics inspection, meets NATO and EU military chiefs",
      "summary": "Commander-in-Chief Syrskyi visited the Pokrovsk sector — the most intensively attacked front with 688 Russian attacks in April — and ordered reinforcement of units. He separately met with NATO and EU Military Committee chairs Dragone and Clancy, announced $25.5 billion in estimated Russian losses from Ukrainian strikes, and warned Russia is advancing across nearly the entire front while its air defense is running short of missiles."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Budanov at Kyiv Security Forum: Ukraine not losing, AI drone breakthrough imminent, no territorial concessions",
      "summary": "Head of the Presidential Office Kyrylo Budanov addressed the 18th Kyiv Security Forum, declaring Ukraine is 'definitely not losing' but needs an AI-driven technological leap to win the drone race. He revealed Ukraine has near-ready autonomous drone systems that will 'surprise' Russia, opposed civilian gun legalization following a recent Kyiv shooting, and reiterated that no Ukrainians will accept loss of any territory in peace talks."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Zelensky visits Saudi Arabia and Azerbaijan, signs security and defense export deals across Gulf region",
      "summary": "President Zelensky made his second visit to Saudi Arabia in under a month, meeting Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman to advance air defense, energy, and infrastructure partnerships. He then traveled to Baku where he signed six agreements including security cooperation with Azerbaijan's President Aliyev. Ukraine has now signed 10-year defense export agreements with Saudi Arabia, UAE, and Qatar focused on drone warfare expertise."
    }
  ]
}

